WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.099 --> 00:00:11.279
So, the blueprint editor can be quite overwhelming at first. The way I like to think about anything

2
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:16.839
inside of Unreal Engine is that whatever programs and software you're used to using,

3
00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:22.480
you'll realize that you generally default to the similar set of buttons for 80%

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.780
of your usage, you'll get used to the flow and you won't use 100% of the buttons

5
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.399
all the time. So, the first thing to keep in mind is don't let the blueprint

6
00:00:31.399 --> 00:00:37.000
editor interface be too overwhelming, we'll dig into it section by section and you'll find

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.560
yourself going to the same bits each time. So, first thing we see is a classic windows

8
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:49.200
text menu where you'll be able to find a whole bunch of additional functionality that

9
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.359
again you may only use 10% of the time but if you've lost any windows or you need

10
00:00:54.359 --> 00:01:00.140
to file save or you need to edit something, they're the kind of text menus that you'll

11
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.739
find up there. The toolbar is something you'll be using quite a lot of the time, you can

12
00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:11.299
compile your blueprints here, you can save your blueprints, you can browse for other

13
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:17.000
assets within the blueprint and set up a few different other properties like class

14
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.939
defaults and things like this. The components window is when we're creating a blueprint,

15
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:28.420
maybe we're creating a player blueprint or maybe we're creating a door blueprint. So,

16
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.900
if we think about the components of a door, maybe we need a door frame, maybe we need

17
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:40.280
a door, maybe we need a box around the door that says the player is within this box.

18
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.299
These are all components of a blueprint and we would add these to the list. It's

19
00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:49.969
about selecting the components that you need to make your blueprint work. In the instance

20
00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:54.750
of a character for example, you might have the character's mesh, you might have a weapon

21
00:01:54.750 --> 00:02:00.189
that the character is holding, you might have some kind of collision that the player has

22
00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:05.750
surrounding them that they can cause to trigger overlap events. Again, components

23
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:11.669
are just a selection of these blueprint functionality that you can create your blueprints

24
00:02:11.669 --> 00:02:19.280
with. In section 4 we have the blueprint panel, this is where you'll be adding variables

25
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.599
and bits of code and you'll be able to see the graph from a higher level about the

26
00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:30.599
kind of inputs that you have here and any kind of main functions. So, think of the blueprint

27
00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:38.240
panel as where most of the data is housed. Now, if we then go into number 5, this is

28
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.319
where all our gameplay scripting actually takes place. You'll see that you can toggle

29
00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:50.639
to the viewport in the top left of section 5 here and the viewport will show you in

30
00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:55.599
a visual representation the components that you have in your blueprint. So again, referencing

31
00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:00.960
section 3 of the list of your components, then in section 5 at the top left the viewport

32
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.840
will help you visualise these components. So if you did select a door with a doorframe,

33
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.560
you'd be able to see these elements in that viewport. The construction script is something

34
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:17.639
we'll get into in a few minutes again. It's a slightly more complex concept but essentially

35
00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:22.719
the construction script will fire every time you compile. So again, those familiar

36
00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:28.599
with C++ will be more familiar with the construction script but even if you're not familiar with

37
00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:34.919
C++, don't worry, we'll dig into that again. And the event graph is where you'll

38
00:03:34.919 --> 00:03:40.360
be spending the majority of your time. It houses all the gameplay code that you need

39
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:45.599
for this specific blueprint 2 function. Don't worry too much about the print text next

40
00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:50.800
to the event graph. The print text is a function that we have inside of this blueprint. You

41
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:57.599
can open the function and edit it and that's more like a quick bar to get to that. So

42
00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:02.800
if I close print text that wouldn't be an issue. We only want to be editing quick

43
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.879
context there and the viewport, the construction script and the event graph are the main

44
00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:12.759
things to worry about there. And then in section 6 we have the details of whatever

45
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.160
we currently have selected. So in this example we've got a variable selected which is a

46
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:22.000
bool and we can edit some of the default properties of this bool. And so you'll be

47
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.600
using the details panel quite a lot in your blueprint journey and it allows you to add

48
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.759
the functionality and customization to the blueprints that you are creating.
